# step4.
Prepare for the restore

Before restoring etcd on your live cluster, we should absolutely check PersistentVolumes (PVs) and PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs).

PV/PVC definitions: Stored in etcd, so they are included in the snapshot.

Actual application data: Stored on disks, NFS, cloud volumes, or other storage backends. The etcd snapshot does not back up this data.

kubectl get pv -o wide

etcd is the cluster’s database. It stores the definitions of every object, including PV and PVC objects, but not the actual data on your disks. When you restore a snapshot, the cluster’s state rolls back to the moment the snapshot was taken, while the real storage (EBS volumes, NFS shares, Ceph, etc.) stays exactly as it is now

What can go wrong after a restore

PVCs created after the snapshot disappear. The pod’s data is still on the underlying disk, but Kubernetes no longer has an object pointing to it.

PVs deleted after the snapshot reappear. They may reference volumes that no longer exist in your cloud or storage backend.
Binding state can be stale. A PV might show as bound to a PVC that no longer exists, or the reverse.

Reclaim policy matters. If a PV had Delete policy and a PVC was deleted after the snapshot, the real disk may already be gone, so the restored PV points to nothing.

Also note the underlying volume IDs and set the reclaim policy to Retain on important PVs, so storage isn’t deleted while you’re mid-recovery


